        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Use the recurrence relation from Step 2 to find a general form for \\(f(n)\\). Start with \\(f(1) = 1\\):\n\\[f(2) = f(1) + 1 + 2 = 1 + 3 = 4,\\]\n\\[f(3) = f(2) + 2 + 2 = 4 + 4 = 8,\\]\n\\[f(4) = f(3) + 3 + 2 = 8 + 5 = 13.\\]\nWe hypothesize that \\(f(n) = \\frac{n^2 + n - 2}{2}\\)."
      },
